// Employee form workflow. Builds the Designation dropdown from the list held on
// the chosen Department, so Production offers Supervisor / Operator / Quality
// Inspector and Store offers Store Person / Packaging Person / Dispatcher.
//
// PASTED TWICE, into two different workflow slots - see the two blocks below.
// On User Input of Department alone is not enough: opening an existing employee
// to edit fires On Load only, so the dropdown would come up with no choices at
// all and saving would blank the designation.
//
// Designation is a plain DROPDOWN with no options configured in the form
// builder. That is correct - every choice it ever shows is added here at
// runtime. It stores plain TEXT, which is what keeps the picker queries in
// getSupervisorMaterials and getProductionWidgetData one-liners with no lookup
// id to resolve first:  Employee[Designation == "Supervisor" && Status == "Active"]
//
// THE API, which is not what it looks like. All three of these are wrong and the
// editor only reports the first one, as "Function applied over invalid
// expression" on line 1:
//   input.Designation.clear()   -> clear is a STATEMENT:  clear Designation;
//   input.Designation.add(nm)   -> a COLON task:          Designation:ui.add(...)
//   ...add(nm) once per name    -> ui.add takes a LIST, and REPLACES the choices
// The field is named bare in both - no "input." prefix - even though reading the
// user's selection on the next line does use it. ui.add and clear are also only
// available in On Load / On User Input / subform add-delete row; they do nothing
// in On Validate, so none of this can be moved there later.
//
// Link names to check against the actual forms before pasting: the Department
// subform is assumed to be `Designations`, its columns `Designation_Name` and
// `Is_Default`.

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. On User Input of Department
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------

clear Designation;

namesList = List();
defName = "";

for each  dRec in Department[ID == input.Department]
{
	for each  dgRow in dRec.Designations
	{
		// A subform text column that was never filled is EMPTY, not null.
		nm = ifnull(dgRow.Designation_Name,"").toString().trim();
		if(nm != "")
		{
			namesList.add(nm);
			if(ifnull(dgRow.Is_Default,false) == true)
			{
				defName = nm;
			}
		}
	}
}

// Guarded - a department with no designations yet must not hand ui.add an
// empty list.
if(namesList.size() > 0)
{
	Designation:ui.add(namesList);
}

// AFTER ui.add, never before: the value has to be one of the choices that now
// exist. Assigning the value does use the input. prefix - only clear and
// ui.add name the field bare.
//
// Unconditional here. Switching department must DROP whatever was selected for
// the old one, or someone moved from Production to Store keeps "Supervisor" and
// stays in the supervisor picker while sitting in a department that has none.
input.Designation = defName;

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. On Load - identical except for the last statement
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// On a NEW record input.Designation is empty and the department default fills
// it, exactly as above. On an EXISTING one it already holds what was saved, and
// that must win - defaulting here would silently rewrite a Quality Inspector to
// Operator every time someone opened the record to correct a phone number.
//
// clear only hides the choices, it does not touch the stored value, so the
// saved designation survives the rebuild above and is still selected once its
// name is added back.
//
//	if(input.Designation == "")
//	{
//		input.Designation = defName;
//	}
